        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Waterloo</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">18 June 1815 (a Sunday); the time the fighting began is uncertain</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The ridge of Mont-Saint-Jean, about 2 kilometres from Waterloo and 15 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, in the municipalities of Braine-l'Alleud and Lasne, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> (then the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The French Imperial Army (Armée du Nord) under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, about 74,500 men with 254 guns; an Anglo-allied army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Wellington</a>, 74,326 men with 156 guns, from the United Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and Nassau; a Prussian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>, of which about 48,000 men in two and a half corps were engaged</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The French army was routed and the Imperial Guard repulsed after the Prussians broke through on the French right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> announced his second abdication on 24 June 1815.</span></div>
