    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> meant to defeat the Prussian and Anglo-allied armies separately, but the Prussians withdrew north to Wavre and marched back to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Wellington</a>, while the 33,000 men Napoleon had sent after them were held up at Wavre.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Wellington</a>'s army stood on a ridge through the afternoon against French infantry, cavalry and artillery, and the Imperial Guard was repulsed in the evening as the Prussians broke through on the French right.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Wellington</a> lost around 17,000 dead or wounded, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> some 7,000 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> 24,000 to 26,000 killed or wounded, with a further 15,000 deserting afterwards.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Wellington</a>'s dispatch put the start of the French attack on Hougoumont at about ten o'clock, other sources say around 11:30, and the historian Andrew Roberts notes that no one is absolutely certain when the battle began.</p></aside>
    </section>

